# i386 overrides - emitted after the profile's fragments, so this can take
# back what a shared fragment turned on. See generate-config.sh.

# Java. OpenJDK 25 cannot be built for 32-bit x86 at all, so runtimes.config's
# BR2_PACKAGE_OPENJDK is withdrawn here rather than on every other arch.
#
# Both routes are closed. The server VM is refused outright, because JDK 24
# removed the 32-bit x86 JIT ports - make/autoconf/basic.m4 stops at
#
#   checking openjdk-target os-cpu... linux-x86
#   configure: error: 32-bit x86 builds are not supported
#
# and that check exempts only --with-jvm-variants=zero. Zero does configure,
# and the target libjvm.so does build, but the build still dies twenty-five
# minutes later:
#
#   Segmentation fault (core dumped) .../buildjdk/hotspot/variant-server/tools/adlc/adlc
#     -DIA32=1 -U_LP64 ... -c .../support/adlc/ad_x86.cpp
#   gmake[3]: *** [make/Main.gmk:624: create-buildjdk-interim-image] Error 2
#
# A 32-bit target on a 64-bit host is a "reduced build" (platform.m4), so
# OpenJDK builds its interim buildjdk as 32-bit x86 as well - and the buildjdk
# is always a server VM, whatever the target's variant. That is the same
# deleted IA32 C2 port, reached the long way round: adlc is C2's ADL compiler,
# and it segfaults. Choosing zero for the target cannot help, because nothing
# about the target's variant reaches the buildjdk.
#
# So this is not a configuration to be found by trying harder - upstream
# removed the port. If Java on i386 is ever wanted, it needs OpenJDK 17 or 21,
# which still carry the x86-32 port, pinned here as a version choice.
#
# Dropping it fits what this architecture is for. i386 exists mainly as the
# 32-bit companion beside an x86_64 image, and the lib32 profile already
# excludes runtimes.config outright, on the grounds that a 32-bit interpreter
# is not part of what the companion promises. This extends the same reasoning
# to the standalone i386 app-sdk.
#
# BR2_PACKAGE_OPENJDK is not set
